          "title": "Kayotsarg",
          "body_md": "**Kayotsarg** (કાઉસ્સગ્ગ) means being completely still — letting go of the body and resting the mind on the soul, often with a silent Logassa or Navkar.",
          "emoji": "🧍"
         },
         {
          "title": "The body's own moves",
          "body_md": "Some things the body does by itself! **Annattha** (અન્નત્થ) lists these exceptions (*agar*): breathing in and out, coughing, sneezing, yawning, burping, passing gas, dizziness, fainting, and tiny movements of the body, of phlegm in the throat and of the eyes. They do not break your kayotsarg.",
          "emoji": "🤧"
         },
         {
          "title": "Real emergencies",
          "body_md": "If there is real danger — like a fire or a snake — you may move to stay safe. Kayotsarg never asks you to be unsafe.",
          "emoji": "🔥"
         },
         {
          "title": "How long?",
          "body_md": "In Samayik: one Logassa silently up to *“chandesu nimmalayara”* (or four Navkar). You finish by saying *“Namo Arihantanam”*. The sutra begins: *“Annattha usasienam, nisasienam…”*",
          "emoji": "⏱️"
         }
        ],
