         {
          "title": "The vow",
          "body_md": "**Karemi Bhante** (કરેમિ ભંતે) is the promise (*pratigna*) that begins your Samayik. Just before it you ask: *“Ichchhakari bhagavan! Pasay kari samayik dandak uchcharavoji”* — please kindly give me the Samayik vow. A guru gives it; if none is present, an elder can — or you say it yourself.",
          "emoji": "📜"
         },
         {
          "title": "First line",
          "body_md": "*“Karemi bhante! Samaiyam savajjam jogam pachchakkhami…”* — Revered one, I take up Samayik and give up all harmful activity (*savadya yog*).",
          "emoji": "🔤"
         },
         {
          "title": "Two ways × three means",
          "body_md": "The vow works in **two ways**: I won't do anything harmful myself, and I won't get anyone else to do it for me. And with **three means**: not with my mind, not with my words, not with my body. Two ways × three means = six doors closed to harm! Your teacher will teach you the sutra's own words.",
          "emoji": "✖️"
         },
         {
          "title": "For how long?",
          "body_md": "The vow lasts for as long as you have promised to stay in Samayik — at least 48 minutes. It ends by turning away from past wrongs, feeling sorry for them and letting them go.",
          "emoji": "⏳"
         }
        ],
        "review": "needs_pathshala_review"
