        "intro": "Touch the glowing spots in order: big toes, knees, wrists, shoulders, top of the head, forehead, throat, heart, navel — nine pujas, thirteen touches. For each pair, Bhagwan's right comes first, then his left. Bhagwan faces you, so his right wrist, knee and shoulder are on your left. The toes are the one surprise: his legs are crossed (padmasana), so his right foot rests on his left thigh and his right big toe is on your right.\n\n🙏 Before you begin: bathe and wear clean clothes that are used only for puja. Put a tilak on your own forehead. Tie a mukhkosh — a folded cloth over your nose and mouth — so your breath does not touch Bhagwan.\n\nChandan puja comes after jal puja (prakshal), once Bhagwan has been gently dried with soft cloths. Take chandan mixed with kesar in a clean bowl and use the ring finger (anamika) of your right hand. Never let your nail touch the murti.\n\nMany people say a short doha in their mind at each place — your Pathshala teacher can teach them. Until then, some families silently say one line of the Navkar at each of the nine places (the same line for both toes, both knees, and so on), from “Namo Arihantanam” at the toes to “Padhamam Havai Mangalam” at the navel.",
